        "math_academy_id": "q-83725",
        "knowledge_point_id": "73571ff9-24a3-52b6-be57-3ef08652ebb7",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding the First or Second Derivative of a Function at a Point Given Its Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial",
        "problem": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=4+3(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{′}(-1)?$",
        "worked_solution": "This is an expansion about the point $x=-1,$ so the coefficient of the first-degree term is ${f}^{′}(-1).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the first-degree term is $3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-1)=3.\n$$",
