      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-83729",
        "knowledge_point_id": "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89",
        "knowledge_point": "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/9fe49372324dac217bb8e96ae6f485fe74308184ec9dd197fc98b973560dd8af.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-2?$",
        "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-2)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-2)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=3+2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}.\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
