      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-152877",
        "knowledge_point_id": "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279",
        "knowledge_point": "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions",
        "problem": "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{x}]}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{3}-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/b9a29cbd8a029e0f26e3da6a042ada0f8a809053233979bb4ff73db3dcf0f10d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
