        "worked_solution": "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{3}{t+1} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-\\frac{3}{(t+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=\\sqrt{t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨{x}^{″}(t),\\,{y}^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{(t+1)^{2}},\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
