        "problem": "${(1-\\frac{5c}{2})}^{6}=$",
        "worked_solution": "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=-\\frac{5c}{2}$ and $n=6$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1-\\frac{5c}{2})}^{6} & =1+6\\cdot (-\\frac{5c}{2})+\\frac{6(6-1)}{2!}\\cdot {(-\\frac{5c}{2})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-15c+\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{25{c}^{2}}{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-15c+15\\cdot \\frac{25{c}^{2}}{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-15c+\\frac{375{c}^{2}}{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
