        "math_academy_id": "q-115060",
        "knowledge_point_id": "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c",
        "knowledge_point": "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number",
        "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{x+2}-4>-2.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+2}-4 & >-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+2} & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+2})}^{2} & >{2}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & >4 \\\\ x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(2,\\infty ).$ The domain of the square root is $x\\ge -2,$ which can be expressed as $[-2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(2,\\infty )\\cap [-2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (2,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>2.$",
