        "problem": "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$, then $F(t)=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int ({e}^{t}-4)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration.",
